Other built-in tags record feature presence while their facilities are
used through module-level functions/types. Their list includes
\texttt{PatternMatch}, \texttt{LazyFeature}, \texttt{Monadic},
\texttt{ResultFeature}, \texttt{CombinatorParser\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{TaskPipeline}, \texttt{EnumReflection}, and the eight shared
facility tags from Chapter 30.

A custom feature can provide a nested mixin:

\begin{code}
struct DomainFeature
{
    mixin template Mixin()
    {
        int domain_value() const { return 17; }
    }
}
struct DomainDSL { mixin DSL!(Pipeline, DomainFeature); }
static assert(HasFeature!(DomainDSL, DomainFeature));
\end{code}

\section{Callable values and alias
callables}\label{ch31-callable-values-and-alias-callables}

Typed functions, delegates and functors can be passed as ordinary
values:

\begin{code}
auto result = 5 | pipe((int x) => x * 2) | pipe((int x) => x + 1);
assert(result == 11);
\end{code}

Generic lambdas can instead be passed as template aliases:

\begin{code}
auto doubleIt = pipe!((x) => x + x)();
assert((4 | doubleIt) == 8);
auto positive = predicate!((x) => x > 0)();
assert(positive(2) && positive(2.5));
\end{code}

\texttt{StaticCallable!f\allowbreak{}n} binds an alias callable to a
concrete functor. Alias factories include
\texttt{pipe!fn(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{predicate!fn(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{when!(\allowbreak{}key,\allowbreak{} fn)\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{otherwise!fn(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{on!(\allowbreak{}tag,\allowbreak{} fn)\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}},
and
\texttt{enum\_\allowbreak{}switch!fn(\allowbreak{}value)\allowbreak{}}.

\section{Pipelines, predicates and
dispatch}\label{ch31-pipelines-predicates-and-dispatch}

Predicates compose through \texttt{\&} and \texttt{|} with short-circuit
evaluation. Use \texttt{\textasciitilde{}} or
\texttt{negated(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} for predicate negation; D's
\texttt{!} produces a boolean.

\begin{code}
auto positive = predicate((int x) => x > 0);
auto even = predicate((int x) => x % 2 == 0);
auto valid = positive & even;
assert(valid(4) && !valid(3));
assert((~positive)(-2));
\end{code}

Dispatch handlers receive the key, followed by any additional arguments:

\begin{code}
auto table = match(
    when!1((int key) => "one"),
    otherwise((int key) => "other"));
assert(table(1) == "one");

auto classify = match(
    when!(pattern!"[0-9]+")((string key) => "number"),
    otherwise((string key) => "other"));
assert(classify("123") == "number");
\end{code}

Handlers must have a common non-void result type. An unmatched key
without an \texttt{otherwise} clause raises an exception.
\texttt{pattern!S} is the small whole-input matcher for literals, dot,
classes/ranges, anchors and \texttt{*}/\texttt{+}; the broader byte-NFA
API is \texttt{StaticRegex!S} in Chapter 33.

\section{Value-type ASTs and bottom-up
rewriting}\label{ch31-value-type-asts-and-bottom-up-rewriting}

String tags are native template parameters:

\begin{code}
auto tree = node!"add"(leaf!"num"(1), leaf!"num"(2));
assert(tree.dump() == "(add (num 1) (num 2))");
auto copy = tree;
copy.child(0) = leaf!"num"(10);
assert(tree.child(0).value() == "1");
\end{code}

\texttt{ASTNode} deeply copies mutable child storage. Its accessors are
\texttt{tag}, \texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}leaf}, \texttt{value},
\texttt{children}, \texttt{child}, \texttt{size}, \texttt{dump}, and
\texttt{toString}.

\begin{code}
import std.conv : to;
auto rules = rewrite_set(rule!"fold-add"(
    (ASTNode n) => n.tag() == "add" && n.size() == 2 &&
                  n.child(0).is_leaf() && n.child(1).is_leaf(),
    (ASTNode n) => leaf!"num"(
        to!int(n.child(0).value()) + to!int(n.child(1).value()))));
assert(rules.apply(tree).dump() == "(num 3)");
\end{code}

Rules run bottom-up and in registration order.
\texttt{max\_\allowbreak{}iterations} defaults to 100; passes stop when
the tree no longer changes. Equality saturation in Chapter 32 retains
multiple equivalent expressions rather than replacing the current tree
on each rule application.

\section{Lazy expressions, literals and
caches}\label{ch31-lazy-expressions-literals-and-caches}

\texttt{terminal(\allowbreak{}value)\allowbreak{}} lifts a scalar or
array into an expression tree. Array expressions evaluate element by
element into one result array; participating arrays must have matching
lengths. Scalars can participate in array operations.

\begin{code}
auto a = terminal([1, 2, 3]);
auto b = terminal([4, 5, 6]);
auto c = terminal([7, 8, 9]);
assert((a + b * c).eval() == [29, 42, 57]);
assert((2 * a).eval() == [2, 4, 6]);
\end{code}

Terminal arrays use D's slice storage semantics. Copying a terminal does
not deep-copy the referenced array. \texttt{ASTNode} explicitly supplies
deep-copy behavior for its child tree.

\begin{code}
auto units = literal_set(
    lit!"_km"((real n) => n * 1000), lit!"_m"((real n) => n));
assert(units.parse("3.5_km") == 3500);
enum distance = literal_set(lit!"_km"((real n) => n * 1000)).parse("3.5_km");
static assert(distance == 3500);
\end{code}

\texttt{memoize!(\allowbreak{}Key,\allowbreak{} Value)\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}fn)\allowbreak{}}
caches results by associative-array key and provides
\texttt{clear\_\allowbreak{}cache}.
\texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}lazy(\allowbreak{}fn)\allowbreak{}} infers
the producer/result types; \texttt{Lazy!T(\allowbreak{}fn)\allowbreak{}}
accepts a delegate/function producer.
\texttt{get(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} and
\texttt{force(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} return the cached value by
reference.
\texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}computed(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} reports
whether production has occurred.

\section{Optional and result flows}\label{ch31-optional-and-result-flows}

\texttt{Maybe!T} supplies \texttt{map}, \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map},
\texttt{filter}, \texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else},
\texttt{has\_\allowbreak{}value}, \texttt{value}, and
\texttt{as\_\allowbreak{}optional}. The module-level optional helpers
accept Phobos \texttt{Nullable!T} values:

\begin{code}
import std.typecons : Nullable;
assert(Maybe!int(5).map((int n) => n + 1).or_else(0) == 6);
assert(map(Nullable!int(3), (int n) => n * 2).get == 6);
\end{code}

\texttt{Result!(\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{} E)\allowbreak{}} explicitly
tags success/error branches, including when \texttt{T} and \texttt{E}
are identical. Use \texttt{from\_\allowbreak{}ok},
\texttt{from\_\allowbreak{}err}, or \texttt{Ok}/\texttt{Err} wrappers.
Operations are \texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}ok},
\texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}err}, \texttt{unwrap},
\texttt{unwrap\_\allowbreak{}err}, \texttt{unwrap\_\allowbreak{}or},
\texttt{map}, \texttt{and\_\allowbreak{}then}, and
\texttt{map\_\allowbreak{}err}. Incorrect unwrap operations raise an
exception.

\begin{code}
alias R = Result!(string, string);
auto ok = R.from_ok("value");
auto err = R.from_err("error");
assert(ok.map((string s) => s.length).unwrap() == 5);
assert(err.map((string s) => s.length).unwrap_err() == "error");
\end{code}

\section{Parsers, semantic actions and
PEG}\label{ch31-parsers-semantic-actions-and-peg}

\texttt{parser(\allowbreak{}fn)\allowbreak{}} wraps a callable taking
\texttt{ref ParsecInput} and returning
\texttt{ExpectedResult!T\allowbreak{}}. Composition preserves concrete
parser/callable types:

{\def\LTcaptype{none} % do not increment counter
\begin{longtable}[]{@{}ll@{}}
\toprule\noalign{}
Operation & D syntax \\
\midrule\noalign{}
\endhead
\bottomrule\noalign{}
\endlastfoot
Sequence & \texttt{a \& b} returns a \texttt{Tuple} \\
Choice & \texttt{a | b} with equal output types \\
Zero-or-more & \texttt{p.\allowbreak{}many(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
/ \texttt{many(\allowbreak{}p)\allowbreak{}} \\
Optional & \texttt{p.\allowbreak{}optional(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
/ \texttt{optional(\allowbreak{}p)\allowbreak{}} \\
Backtracking &
\texttt{try\_\allowbreak{}parse(\allowbreak{}p)\allowbreak{}} \\
Expected-label replacement &
\texttt{labeled(\allowbreak{}p,\allowbreak{} label)\allowbreak{}} \\
Whole-source execution &
\texttt{run\_\allowbreak{}parser(\allowbreak{}p,\allowbreak{} source)\allowbreak{}} \\
\end{longtable}
}

\begin{code}
auto pair = ch('a') & ch('b');
auto alternate = try_parse(pair) | (ch('a') & ch('c'));
assert(!run_parser(alternate, "ac").value.isNull);
auto digits = satisfy((char c) => c >= '0' && c <= '9', "digit").many();
assert(run_parser(digits, "123").value.get == ['1', '2', '3']);
\end{code}

Inspect \texttt{value.\allowbreak{}isNull} and \texttt{error} on a
\texttt{ParseOutcome}. Failures distinguish \texttt{Soft} and
\texttt{Committed}; \texttt{try\_\allowbreak{}parse} restores the cursor
and softens a failure. Repetition rejects successful parsers that
consume no input. \texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}with\_\allowbreak{}action}
passes a \texttt{ParserStage} span to a user callback;
\texttt{production!id(\allowbreak{}fn)\allowbreak{}} stores a
compile-time production identifier with its handler.

PEG combinators are \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}seq},
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}choice}, \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}opt},
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}many}, \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}many1},
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}and}, \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}not},
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}rule}, and \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}channel}.
\texttt{create\_\allowbreak{}peg\_\allowbreak{}definition(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
and
\texttt{derive\_\allowbreak{}peg\_\allowbreak{}definition(\allowbreak{}parent)\allowbreak{}}
return reference-type definitions.
\texttt{add\_\allowbreak{}rule!pattern(\allowbreak{}action)\allowbreak{}}
returns a stable rule reference. Channels, semantic actions and fluent
matcher arms follow Chapters 27--28, using D callbacks such as
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}ref PEGMatch m)\allowbreak{} \{ .\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{} \}}.
PEG patterns retain their documented greedy prefix semantics.

\section{Tasks and shared state}\label{ch31-tasks-and-shared-state}

\texttt{Task(\allowbreak{}name,\allowbreak{} callback)\allowbreak{}}
takes a \texttt{ref TaskState} callback returning
\texttt{Result!(\allowbreak{}string,\allowbreak{} string)\allowbreak{}}.
\texttt{task1 | task2} creates a \texttt{TaskChain}; \texttt{run}
records each result by task name. Policies are \texttt{StopOnError} and
\texttt{ContinueOnError}.
\texttt{suspend(\allowbreak{}state)\allowbreak{}} stops subsequent work;
\texttt{upon} runs a task when a supplied event callable returns true.
Stateful caches, lazy values and PEG objects do not provide
synchronization.

\section{Native enum reflection}\label{ch31-native-enum-reflection}

\begin{code}
enum Color { Red = -1000, Green = 0, AliasGreen = 0, Blue = 1000 }
static assert(enum_count!Color() == 3);
static assert(enum_name!(Color.Red)() == "Red");
static assert(enum_values!Color() == [Color.Red, Color.Green, Color.Blue]);
auto blue = enum_cast!Color("blue", case_insensitive);
assert(blue.get == Color.Blue);
auto name = enum_switch!((c) => enum_name!(typeof(c).value)())(Color.Blue);
assert(name == "Blue");
\end{code}

Declared members are reflected without numeric search-range limits.
Values are sorted numerically and duplicate values use the first
declared name. Checked casts/indexing and adjacency return
\texttt{Nullable}; arrays of values, names and entries are returned as
fixed-size values.
\texttt{enum\_\allowbreak{}reflected!E(\allowbreak{}integer)\allowbreak{}}
checks reflected membership in D. \texttt{enum\_\allowbreak{}switch} has
an optional fallback; its no-fallback form raises an exception for an
unknown value.

\begin{code}
@(EnumOptions(true)) enum Access { Read = 1, Write = 2, Execute = 4, All = 7 }
assert(enum_flags_name(Access.Read | Access.Write) == "Read|Write");
@(EnumOptions(false, 2)) @(EnumNames(["c_Red": "rouge"]))
enum Named { c_Red, c_Blue }
static assert(enum_name(Named.c_Red) == "rouge");
static assert(enum_name(Named.c_Blue) == "Blue");
\end{code}

\texttt{EnumOptions} sets flags mode and prefix trimming;
\texttt{EnumNames} maps declaration names to display names. Flags
reflection selects single-bit members. Helpers include
\texttt{enum\_\allowbreak{}flags\_\allowbreak{}cast},
\texttt{enum\_\allowbreak{}flags\_\allowbreak{}contains},
\texttt{enum\_\allowbreak{}flags\_\allowbreak{}test}, and
\texttt{enum\_\allowbreak{}flags\_\allowbreak{}test\_\allowbreak{}any}.
\texttt{EnumArray!(\allowbreak{}E,\allowbreak{} V)\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{EnumBitset!E}, and \texttt{EnumSet!E} supply enum-indexed
containers. D's native enum formatting/bitwise operators supply the
host-language integrations. Chapter 29 documents C++'s upstream-specific
configuration, namespace aliases and stream/formatter APIs.

\section{Verify the D module}\label{ch31-verify-the-d-module}

\begin{code}
ldc2 -unittest -main MuDSL.d -of=mudsl-test
./mudsl-test
dmd -unittest -main MuDSL.d -of=mudsl-test-dmd
./mudsl-test-dmd
gdc -funittest -fmain MuDSL.d -o mudsl-test-gdc
./mudsl-test-gdc
\end{code}

The tests cover established features and the new shared facilities.
Build the integration example with
\texttt{ldc2 -I.\allowbreak{} MuDSL.\allowbreak{}d examples/\allowbreak{}26-equality-satu\allowbreak{}ration.\allowbreak{}d -of=eqsat-exam\allowbreak{}ple},
then run \texttt{.\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}eqsat-example}.
